d tells us that that ratio difference  is really  something that we're going to tease out  and try to think about more  ways in which we can use this  information to help understand the  biochemistry that's happening  so katie wrote an excellent  method development article about this to  show you how and  all the whole process uh that went  through and in more detail the methods  that go into this so i  encourage you to go look and look at  that method  review it and and um  ask any questions that you might have  about what happens and how you can  detect  weapons from uh chemical weapons and  nerve agents it's a very intriguing  article and the previous review article  also has a lot of information about what  might happen  if we can start to do more scale up  screening especially in places like  syria where  we had a a potential chemical attack but  we don't have any way to confirm that  if we could allow a forensic  anthropologist to go in collect a little  bit of samples we might be able to  confirm  this kind of scenario  so as a review we developed a model to  understand incorporation of toxic  substances into bone  we developed an instrument method and  sample preparation methods to go along  with that  a lot of validation went in this and you  can again review that article in  forensic toxicology to  to get all the details that went  involved we  we were in the end able to analyze  samples from minifigs exposed to nerve  agents  and found that empa was found in  multiple samples using this triple quad  approach  and this would really be represent the  first time that this kind of exposure  has ever been detected in skeletal  remains  so it represents hopefully we think as a  good future forward for analytical  chemistry and forensic toxicology and  forensic anthropology  but beyond that there's also some  interest there's also potential that  maybe this kind of analysis in bone  would be important  for understanding uh maybe cancer  therapies or other unders