tion  uh there were the there aren't many  methods out there for extraction  of these species from bone and  even the extraction of these species  from blood can be  can be difficult at times and so and  bone presents a very unique kind of  matrix that mixes between both solid  structure meaning very difficult to  homogenize and grind  to get a a medium that you can extract  from so we had to optimize how you  pulverize the bone and different kinds  of bone  it will have different strengths so that  kind of pulverization might vary  depending on the type of bone you're  analyzing  and then we also had that had to verify  all across the  approach that the extraction from that  kind of pulverized material would be  a valid approach for doing extraction so  we spent a lot of time testing different  modes of extraction  testing different methods that would  allow us to  to know that we're detecting what's  there and then once we get to that point  and analyze real samples the idea of ion  suppression comes in  because we're looking at polar species  that are related to  those that are present in the bone like  citrate  we have to optimize the extraction to  maintain those species in the extraction  but also remove those contaminants and i  said citrate  was one of those contaminants that was  important so the addition of sodium  hydroxide in the extraction process  helped  reduce some of those salts but we could  never really fully eliminate them so we  still have to deal with the ion  suppression  even in an optimized extraction approach  and so i think  as other people start to work on this  and new methods come out maybe that'll  get better  um but hopefully we showed you that it's  even  even with the amount of ion suppression  present it's still possible to have a  very sensitive  method and valid method to analyze and  extract and confirm that these are  present  so that we can look in the future of new  studies and hopefully  gain some insight into more  human re